{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EeMpvos7bhC1p2G39thFvz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011TkdnuDtk1p1vDsjfXvBao","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","subagent_type":"claude","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue paper review. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Review the paper below and provide a detailed assessment. Do NOT assign a final score — that happens in the roundtable. Do NOT speculate about tier/quality of results.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Hidden States Universally Encode Safety Commitment Better Than Text: A Cross-Architecture Evaluation of Reasoning Model Monitoring\"\n\nThe paper studies \"self-jailbreak\" in reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1, QwQ), where extended CoT reasoning culminates in a \"commitment point\" (CP) where safety reasoning permanently ceases. It compares hidden-state probes, text-based monitors, and unsupervised geometric methods across 4 models (2 architectures × 2 scales: r1-8b Llama, r1-32b Qwen2.5, QwQ-32B Qwen2.5, OpenThinker-7B Qwen2.5).\n\nThree main claims:\n1. Hidden-state temporal probes achieve pre-CP FPR 14–16% vs 27–36% for text (pooled p=0.003, I²=0.12)\n2. Temporal context (W=15) universally improves coverage by 5–7pp regardless of modality\n3. Pre-textual detection remains fundamentally unreliable (median lead=0, only 38–45% positive lead)\n\nAdditional finding: Cross-model transfer achieves 67–74% accuracy (vs 50% chance), suggesting shared safety geometry.\n\n## Key Experimental Details\n- 209 traces per model from HarmThoughts dataset (166 train / 43 test)\n- Hidden-state probes: static LR, temporal LR (W=15), GRU (h=256, 7 layers, 5-seed)\n- Text probes: TF-IDF + identical probe architectures\n- Baselines: TrajGuard/Mahalanobis, BOCPD\n- Cross-transfer uses learned linear projection (input→256→target)\n- 1000 bootstrap resamples for CIs\n- Metrics: Coverage, Pre-CP FPR, Precision, Lead time, Safe FPR\n\n## Key Tables from Paper\n\nTable 1 (Static probes): r1-8b L14 44% acc=.817 cov=60.5% FPR_pre=16.2% prec=62.2%; r1-32b L28 44% acc=.824 cov=51.2% FPR_pre=14.8% prec=63.4%; QwQ-32B L30 47% acc=.809 cov=48.8% FPR_pre=15.5% prec=61.2%\n\nTable 2 (Temporal vs static): All models show +4.6 to +7.0pp coverage gain with temporal W=15. FPR_pre stays 14–16%.\n\nTable 3 (Cross-transfer): Cross-family 67–71%, within-family 73–74%, all above 50% chance.\n\nTable 4 (Lead time): Mean lead +4.7 to +6.2, median 0 or -1, %positive 38–45%.\n\nTable 5 (TrajGuard failure): Max delta between safe/jb MD < 0.025 across all models.\n\nTable 6 (Pooled bootstrap): HS vs Text FPR_pre: -14.2pp, p=.003, I²=.12. HS vs Text precision: +12.4pp, p=.001, I²=.08. Temporal vs static cov: +5.8pp, p=.028, I²=.21. GRU vs LR lead: +2.1 steps, p=.14, I²=.31.\n\nTable 7 (Oracle decomposition): 62% future labels, 28% multi-step trajectory, 10% global context.\n\nTable 8 (2×2 matrix): OpenThinker-7B FPR_pre=16.5%, prec=57.1%, Δtemp=+5.8pp. Near-parallel effects.\n\nTable 9 (Window ablation): Coverage saturates at W=15-20, FPR_pre stable 14.5–15.8%.\n\nTable 10 (Layer ablation): Peak at 44-47% depth, broad plateau.\n\nTable 11 (16-label): Modest +2-3pp improvement over binary.\n\nTable 12 (GRU vs LR 32B): GRU cov 78-81% but safe FPR 58-61% vs LR safe FPR 35-37%.\n\n## Review Context (Imagined Experiments from Design)\nThe experimental plan called for 12 experiments (exp-001 through exp-012). The paper covers:\n- exp-001 (cross-arch static): ✓ Table 1\n- exp-002 (cross-arch temporal): ✓ Table 2\n- exp-003 (cross-transfer): ✓ Table 3\n- exp-004 (lead time): ✓ Table 4\n- exp-005 (TrajGuard failure): ✓ Table 5\n- exp-006 (bootstrap CIs): ✓ Table 6 + Appendix A\n- exp-007 (window ablation): ✓ Table 9\n- exp-008 (layer ablation): ✓ Table 10\n- exp-009 (16-label): ✓ Table 11\n- exp-010 (oracle decomposition): ✓ Table 7\n- exp-011 (OpenThinker 2×2): ✓ Table 8\n- exp-012 (GRU vs LR 32B): ✓ Table 12\n\nAll 12 planned experiments appear covered.\n\n## Your Review Focus (Methods + Experiments)\n\nPlease evaluate:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Are the probe designs, baselines, and evaluation metrics well-justified? Any methodological flaws?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor**: \n   - Is 209 traces / 43 test traces per model sufficient?\n   - Are the bootstrap/pooling approaches statistically valid?\n   - Are there confounds in the cross-architecture comparison?\n   - Is the TF-IDF text baseline fair? Could stronger text representations change the conclusion?\n   - Is the cross-transfer projection head (linear) sufficient or could it be a bottleneck?\n\n3. **Claim support**: Do the experiments actually support the three claims? Are there gaps?\n\n4. **Missing experiments or controls**: What experiments would strengthen the paper?\n\n5. **Presentation of results**: Are tables clear? Is information density appropriate?\n\n6. **Reproducibility concerns**\n\nProvide your assessment in structured form with strengths, weaknesses, questions for authors, and a preliminary score tendency (lean accept/reject/borderline) with justification. Be thorough and critical but fair."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":38646,"cache_read_input_tokens":24203,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":38646},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"584eac82-aa50-4806-9859-0d2ba8093b1f","uuid":"ae501b56-6af5-4d45-be3b-68ae6cd62206"}
