{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EeMpvos7bhC1p2G39thFvz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PuEaNkgPr1TynPWsHHb6aQ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","subagent_type":"claude","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue paper review. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Review the paper below and provide a detailed assessment. Do NOT assign a final score — that happens in the roundtable. Do NOT speculate about tier/quality of results.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Hidden States Universally Encode Safety Commitment Better Than Text: A Cross-Architecture Evaluation of Reasoning Model Monitoring\"\n\nThe paper studies \"self-jailbreak\" in reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1, QwQ), where extended CoT reasoning culminates in a \"commitment point\" (CP) where safety reasoning permanently ceases. It compares hidden-state probes, text-based monitors, and unsupervised geometric methods across 4 models (2 architectures × 2 scales: r1-8b Llama, r1-32b Qwen2.5, QwQ-32B Qwen2.5, OpenThinker-7B Qwen2.5).\n\nThree main claims:\n1. Hidden-state temporal probes achieve pre-CP FPR 14–16% vs 27–36% for text (pooled p=0.003, I²=0.12)\n2. Temporal context (W=15) universally improves coverage by 5–7pp regardless of modality\n3. Pre-textual detection remains fundamentally unreliable (median lead=0, only 38–45% positive lead)\n\nAdditional finding: Cross-model transfer achieves 67–74% accuracy (vs 50% chance), suggesting shared safety geometry.\n\n## Paper Structure\n- Section 1 (Introduction): Motivation from self-jailbreak in reasoning models. Three claims listed. Mentions 4 models, 2 arch families, 2 scales.\n- Section 2 (Related Work): Three subsections: hidden-state safety detection (TrajGuard, SafeSwitch, SIREN, RepE, refusal directions), reasoning model safety (HarmThoughts, Self-Jailbreaking, SafeRemind, SafeThink), change-point detection (BOCPD, CUSUM, PRMs).\n- Section 3 (Problem Setup): Defines CP formally, detection task, metrics (Coverage, Pre-CP FPR, Precision, Lead time, Safe FPR), 4 models, HarmThoughts data.\n- Section 4 (Methods): Static LR, Temporal LR W=15, GRU h=256 7-layer, Text TF-IDF probes, TrajGuard/Mahalanobis baseline, BOCPD baseline, cross-model transfer with projection head.\n- Section 5 (Experiments): 8 subsections covering all main experiments.\n- Section 6 (Ablations): Window size, layer selection, 16-label supervision, GRU vs LR.\n- Section 7 (Discussion): Universality, practical implications, fundamental limits, limitations.\n- Section 8 (Conclusion)\n- References: 13 citations\n- Appendix A: Confidence intervals\n\n## Related Work Citations\nThe paper cites:\n1. DeepSeek-AI 2025 (DeepSeek-R1)\n2. Qwen 2025 (QwQ)\n3. Zhang et al. 2024 (HarmThoughts)\n4. Li et al. 2024 (Self-Jailbreaking)\n5. Wang et al. 2024 (TrajGuard)\n6. Chen et al. 2024 (SafeSwitch)\n7. Liu et al. 2024 (SIREN)\n8. Zou et al. 2024 (Representation Engineering)\n9. Arditi et al. 2024 (Refusal directions)\n10. Adams & MacKay 2007 (BOCPD)\n11. Page 1954 (CUSUM)\n12. Lightman et al. 2023 (PRMs)\n13. Xie et al. 2025 (SafeRemind)\n14. Wu et al. 2025 (SafeThink)\n\n## Your Review Focus (Novelty + Related Work)\n\nPlease evaluate:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**:\n   - Is studying commitment point detection in reasoning models genuinely novel?\n   - Is the \"hidden states beat text\" finding surprising or expected?\n   - Is the cross-architecture transfer finding novel and significant?\n   - Is the \"pre-textual detection is fundamentally unreliable\" finding novel?\n   - How does this compare to existing representation probing literature (probing classifiers, concept probes, etc.)?\n   - Is this more of a systematic empirical study or does it offer new methods/insights?\n\n2. **Related work completeness**:\n   - Are important related works missing? Consider: probing classifiers literature (Belinkov, Conneau, etc.), activation patching/causal tracing (Meng et al.), steering vectors, latent space analysis, process supervision beyond PRMs.\n   - Is 13 citations sufficient for a top venue paper?\n   - Does the Related Work adequately position the paper against prior art?\n\n3. **Significance and impact**:\n   - How impactful are the findings for the safety community?\n   - Is the \"universal property\" claim justified with 4 models (3 Qwen2.5-based)?\n   - Does the paper advance understanding or just confirm expected results?\n\n4. **Framing and positioning**:\n   - Is the title justified by the evidence?\n   - Are the claims appropriately scoped?\n   - Is the paper more of an empirical study or does it make conceptual contributions?\n\n5. **Writing quality**:\n   - Is the paper well-written for the target audience?\n   - Are there structural issues?\n   - Any AI writing style markers (dashes, -ing phrases, etc.)?\n\nProvide your assessment in structured form with strengths, weaknesses, questions for authors, and a preliminary score tendency (lean accept/reject/borderline) with justification. Be thorough and critical but fair."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":38646,"cache_read_input_tokens":24203,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":38646},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"584eac82-aa50-4806-9859-0d2ba8093b1f","uuid":"c618e25f-1f0c-4feb-a5cb-c7ad09ec223e"}
