FPR_pre 14–16%.\nTable 3 (Cross-transfer): Cross-family 67–71%, within-family 73–74%.\nTable 4 (Lead time): Mean +4.7 to +6.2, median 0/-1, %positive 38–45%.\nTable 5 (TrajGuard failure): Max delta < 0.025 across models.\nTable 6 (Pooled bootstrap): HS vs Text FPR_pre: -14.2pp, p=.003, I²=.12.\nTable 7 (Oracle decomp): 62% future labels, 28% trajectory, 10% global.\nTable 8 (2×2 matrix): Near-parallel effects, neither arch nor scale dominates.\nTable 9 (Window ablation): Coverage saturates at W=15-20.\nTable 10 (Layer ablation): Peak at 44-47% depth.\nTable 11 (16-label): Modest +2-3pp improvement.\nTable 12 (GRU vs LR 32B): GRU cov 78-81% but safe FPR 58-61%.\nCIs (Appendix A): r1-32b temporal coverage [0.442, 0.698], pre-CP FPR [0.082, 0.215], precision [0.498, 0.712].\n\n## Planned Experiments Coverage\nAll 12 planned experiments appear covered (exp-001 through exp-012).\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate thoroughly on methods and experiments:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Probe designs, baselines, evaluation metrics well-justified? Flaws?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: \n   - 43 test traces per model sufficient?\n   - Bootstrap/pooling statistically valid?\n   - Confounds in cross-architecture comparison?\n   - TF-IDF text baseline fair? Could neural text embeddings change conclusion?\n   - Cross-transfer linear projection sufficient or bottleneck?\n3. **Claim support**: Do experiments support the three claims? Gaps?\n4. **Missing experiments/controls**: What would strengthen the paper?\n5. **Result presentation**: Tables clear? Information density appropriate?\n6. **Reproducibility**\n\nProvide: Strengths (bullet list), Weaknesses (bullet list), Questions for Authors (numbered), Preliminary tendency (lean accept/reject/borderline) with justification.","uuid":"c1c461bc-0af7-48bc-b1d0-a46a52a77671","session_id":"584eac82-aa50-4806-9859-0d2ba8093b1f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue paper review. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Provide a detailed assessment. Do NOT assign a final score — that happens in the roundtable.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Hidden States Universally Encode Safety Commitment Better Than Text: A Cross-Architecture Evaluation of Reasoning Model Monitoring\"\n\nThe paper studies \"self-jailbreak\" in reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1, QwQ), where extended CoT reasoning culminates in a \"commitment point\" (CP) where safety reasoning permanently ceases. It compares hidden-state probes, text-based monitors, and unsupervised geometric methods across 4 models (2 architectures × 2 scales: r1-8b Llama, r1-32b Qwen2.5, QwQ-32B Qwen2.5, OpenThinker-7B Qwen2.5).\n\nThree main claims:\n1. Hidden-state temporal probes achieve pre-CP FPR 14–16% vs 27–36% for text (pooled p=0.003, I²=0.12)\n2. Temporal context (W=15) universally improves coverage by 5–7pp regardless of modality\n3. Pre-textual detection remains fundamentally unreliable (median lead=0, only 38–45% positive lead)\n\nAdditional: Cross-model transfer achieves 67–74% accuracy (vs 50% chance).\n\n## Key Experimental Details\n- 209 traces per model from HarmThoughts dataset (166 train / 43 test)\n- Hidden-state probes: static LR, temporal LR (W=15), GRU (h=256, 7 layers, 5-seed)\n- Text probes: TF-IDF + identical probe architectures\n- Baselines: TrajGuard/Mahalanobis, BOCPD\n- Cross-transfer uses learned linear projection (input→256→target)\n- 1000 bootstrap resamples for CIs\n\n## Key Results\nTable 1 (Static probes): r1-8b acc=.817 cov=60.5% FPR_pre=16.2% prec=62.2%; r1-32b acc=.824 cov=51.2% FPR_pre=14.8% prec=63.4%; QwQ-32B acc=.809 cov=48.8% FPR_pre=15.5% prec=61.2%\nTable 2 (Temporal vs static): +4.6 to +7.0pp coverage, FPR_pre 14–16%.\nTable 3 (Cross-transfer): Cross-family 67–71%, within-family 73–74%.\nTable 4 (Lead time): Mean +4.7 to +6.2, median 0/-1, %positive 38–45%.\nTable 5 (TrajGuard failure): Max delta < 0.025 across models.\nTable 6 (Pooled bootstrap): HS vs Text FPR_pre: -14.2pp, p=.003, I²=.12.\nTable 7 (Oracle decomp): 62% future labels, 28% trajectory, 10% global.\nTable 8 (2×2 matrix): Near-parallel effects, neither arch nor scale dominates.\nTable 9 (Window ablation): Coverage saturates at W=15-20.\nTable 10 (Layer ablation): Peak at 44-47% depth.\nTable 11 (16-label): Modest +2-3pp improvement.\nTable 12 (GRU vs LR 32B): GRU cov 78-81% but safe FPR 58-61%.\nCIs (Appendix A): r1-32b temporal coverage [0.442, 0.698], pre-CP FPR [0.082, 0.215], precision [0.498, 0.712].\n\n## Planned Experiments Coverage\nAll 12 planned experiments appear covered (exp-001 through exp-012).\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate thoroughly on methods and experiments:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Probe designs, baselines, evaluation metrics well-justified? Flaws?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: \n   - 43 test traces per model sufficient?\n   - Bootstrap/pooling statistically valid?\n   - Confounds in cross-architecture comparison?\n   - TF-IDF text baseline fair? Could neural text embeddings change conclusion?\n   - Cross-transfer linear projection sufficient or bottleneck?\n3. **Claim support**: Do experiments support the three claims? Gaps?\n4. **Missing experiments/controls**: What would strengthen the paper?\n5. **Result presentation**: Tables clear? Information density appropriate?\n6. **Reproducibility**\n\nProvide: Strengths (bullet list), Weaknesses (bullet list), Questions for Authors (numbered), Preliminary tendency (lean accept/reject/borderline) with justification."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MYPrkjHZ1hJynCtMmTH3xh","session_id":"584eac82-aa50-4806-9859-0d2ba8093b1f","uuid":"0e1c93d1-6ad9-40e5-89d3-a763da055b90","timestamp":"2026-05-16T21:52:05.266Z"}
