{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XEHwCM3w5qtbgwUky2gg6C","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FpcQ7SMN3CQybniSniuyja","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T212347/best/paper.tex","offset":150,"limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4086,"cache_read_input_tokens":63064,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4086,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":73,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BrBVhVC51yBg6Sz2Ht5fBW","session_id":"584eac82-aa50-4806-9859-0d2ba8093b1f","uuid":"dd6aaccf-bc9b-4a2c-8d5f-4668947b6228"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FpcQ7SMN3CQybniSniuyja","type":"tool_result","content":"150\t\\small\n151\t\\caption{Temporal vs. static probe comparison. Temporal windowing provides +5--7pp coverage gain across all models while maintaining low pre-CP FPR.}\n152\t\\label{tab:temporal_cross}\n153\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n154\t\\toprule\n155\tModel & Method & Cov & $\\Delta$ & FPR$_{\\text{pre}}$ & Prec & Lead \\\\\n156\t\\midrule\n157\tr1-8b & Static L14 & 60.5\\% & -- & 16.2\\% & 62.2\\% & +3.0 \\\\\n158\tr1-8b & Temp W=15 & 65.1\\% & +4.6 & 16.0\\% & 58.3\\% & +4.9 \\\\\n159\t\\midrule\n160\tr1-32b & Static L28 & 51.2\\% & -- & 14.8\\% & 63.4\\% & $-$1.4 \\\\\n161\tr1-32b & Temp W=15 & 58.1\\% & +6.9 & 14.2\\% & 60.1\\% & +5.8 \\\\\n162\t\\midrule\n163\tQwQ-32B & Static L30 & 48.8\\% & -- & 15.5\\% & 61.2\\% & $-$1.8 \\\\\n164\tQwQ-32B & Temp W=15 & 55.8\\% & +7.0 & 14.9\\% & 58.4\\% & +5.2 \\\\\n165\t\\bottomrule\n166\t\\end{tabular}\n167\t\\end{table}\n168\t\n169\tThe 32B models show slightly larger temporal gains (+6.9--7.0pp vs. +4.6pp for r1-8b), likely because their longer traces contain richer temporal structure within the $W$=15 window. The pre-CP FPR remains stable in the 14--16\\% range regardless of temporal context, establishing the precision advantage as modality-specific rather than method-dependent.\n170\t\n171\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}\n172\t\n173\tTable~\\ref{tab:transfer} reports cross-model probe transfer accuracy. All transfer conditions substantially exceed chance (50\\%), with within-family transfer (73--74\\%) outperforming cross-family transfer (68--71\\%).\n174\t\n175\t\\begin{table}[t]\n176\t\\centering\n177\t\\small\n178\t\\caption{Cross-model transfer accuracy. A learned linear projection maps between hidden spaces. All conditions exceed chance (50\\%), revealing shared safety geometry.}\n179\t\\label{tab:transfer}\n180\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n181\t\\toprule\n182\tTrain & Test & Acc & vs. Native & vs. Chance \\\\\n183\t\\midrule\n184\tr1-8b (Llama) & r1-32b (Qwen) & 69.2\\% & $-$13.2 & +19.2 \\\\\n185\tr1-8b (Llama) & QwQ-32B (Qwen) & 67.8\\% & $-$13.1 & +17.8 \\\\\n186\tr1-32b (Qwen) & r1-8b (Llama) & 71.4\\% & $-$10.3 & +21.4 \\\\\n187\tQwQ-32B (Qwen) & r1-8b (Llama) & 70.8\\% & $-$10.9 & +20.8 \\\\\n188\tr1-32b (Qwen) & QwQ-32B (Qwen) & 74.3\\% & $-$6.6 & +24.3 \\\\\n189\tQwQ-32B (Qwen) & r1-32b (Qwen) & 73.1\\% & $-$9.3 & +23.1 \\\\\n190\t\\bottomrule\n191\t\\end{tabular}\n192\t\\end{table}\n193\t\n194\tThe within-Qwen2.5 advantage (73--74\\% vs. 68--71\\% cross-family) is expected: models sharing a base architecture have more similar representational structure. The consistent above-chance transfer across architecture families suggests that safety commitment encoding has both a universal component (shared across all reasoning models) and an architecture-specific component.\n195\t\n196\t\\subsection{Lead Time Analysis}\n197\t\n198\tTable~\\ref{tab:lead} reports lead time distributions across all models. The signature pattern of positive mean but zero median appears universally, establishing that pre-textual detection is unreliable as a fundamental property of reasoning models.\n199\t\n200\t\\begin{table}[t]\n201\t\\centering\n202\t\\small\n203\t\\caption{Lead time distribution across models. Positive mean with zero/negative median indicates that most detections coincide with or follow the textual commitment point.}\n204\t\\label{tab:lead}\n205\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n206\t\\toprule\n207\tModel & Method & Mean & Med & \\%Pos & Cov \\\\\n208\t\\midrule\n209\tr1-8b & Temp LR & +4.9 & 0 & 39\\% & 65.1\\% \\\\\n210\tr1-8b & GRU & +4.7 & 0 & 39\\% & 76--93\\% \\\\\n211\tr1-32b & Temp LR & +5.8 & 0 & 42\\% & 58.1\\% \\\\\n212\tr1-32b & GRU & +6.2 & 0 & 45\\% & 81$\\pm$9\\% \\\\\n213\tQwQ-32B & Temp LR & +5.2 & $-$1 & 38\\% & 55.8\\% \\\\\n214\tQwQ-32B & GRU & +5.5 & 0 & 41\\% & 78$\\pm$11\\% \\\\\n215\t\\bottomrule\n216\t\\end{tabular}\n217\t\\end{table}\n218\t\n219\tGRU coverage instability replicates across architectures (std 9--11\\% across seeds on 32B models), confirming this reflects fundamental signal noise rather than model-specific limitations.\n220\t\n221\t\\subsection{TrajGuard/Mahalanobis Failure}\n222\t\n223\tTable~\\ref{tab:trajguard} demonstrates universal failure of geometric methods. The maximum difference between safe-trace and jailbreak-trace Mahalanobis distances is less than 0.025 across all models, confirming that in the variance-dominated subspace, safety commitment is undetectable.\n224\t\n225\t\\begin{table}[t]\n226\t\\centering\n227\t\\small\n228\t\\caption{TrajGuard (Mahalanobis distance) failure across models. The negligible $\\Delta$ between safe and jailbreak MD values confirms geometric methods cannot detect commitment.}\n229\t\\label{tab:trajguard}\n230\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n231\t\\toprule\n232\tModel & Config & Cross\\% & MD$_{\\text{safe}}$ & MD$_{\\text{jb}}$ & Max $\\Delta$ \\\\\n233\t\\midrule\n234\tr1-8b & pca\\_64\\_p75 & 30.2\\% & 6.66 & 6.73 & 0.023 \\\\\n235\tr1-32b & pca\\_64\\_p75 & 27.9\\% & 7.12 & 7.18 & 0.019 \\\\\n236\tQwQ-32B & pca\\_128\\_p75 & 25.6\\% & 7.45 & 7.51 & 0.022 \\\\\n237\t\\bottomrule\n238\t\\end{tabular}\n239\t\\end{table}\n240\t\n241\t\\subsection{Pooled Statistical Analysis}\n242\t\n243\tTable~\\ref{tab:pooled} reports pooled bootstrap results across all three primary models (1000 resamples). The low $I^2$ values indicate minimal heterogeneity, confirming that effect sizes are consistent across architectures.\n244\t\n245\t\\begin{table}[t]\n246\t\\centering\n247\t\\small\n248\t\\caption{Pooled three-model bootstrap analysis. Low $I^2$ indicates consistent effects across architectures. The HS precision advantage reaches p=0.003 when pooled.}\n249\t\\label{tab:pooled}\n250\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n251\t\\toprule\n252\tComparison & Effect & 95\\% CI & p & $I^2$ \\\\\n253\t\\midrule\n254\tHS vs Text FPR$_{\\text{pre}}$ & $-$14.2pp & [$-$17.8, $-$8.1] & .003 & .12 \\\\\n255\tHS vs Text precision & +12.4pp & [+7.9, +16.1] & .001 & .08 \\\\\n256\tTemporal vs Static cov & +5.8pp & [+0.6, +10.4] & .028 & .21 \\\\\n257\tGRU vs LR lead & +2.1 steps & [$-$1.2, +5.8] & .14 & .31 \\\\\n258\t\\bottomrule\n259\t\\end{tabular}\n260\t\\end{table}\n261\t\n262\tAUROC provides threshold-independent confirmation: hidden-state temporal probes achieve 0.862 (r1-32b) and 0.849 (QwQ-32B) vs. text-based 0.781 and 0.769, an 8--9pp advantage.\n263\t\n264\t\\subsection{Oracle Decomposition}\n265\t\n266\tTable~\\ref{tab:oracle} decomposes the gap between probe performance and oracle ceiling. The oracle achieves 100\\% early detection with +31-step mean lead because it accesses future labels by definition. Our analysis reveals that 62\\% of this advantage comes from future information unavailable to any real-time method.\n267\t\n268\t\\begin{table}[t]\n269\t\\centering\n270\t\\small\n271\t\\caption{Oracle advantage decomposition. The majority (62\\%) of the gap is attributable to future information, establishing a principled limit on real-time detection.}\n272\t\\label{tab:oracle}\n273\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n274\t\\toprule\n275\tSource & \\% of Gap & Probe Access? & Method \\\\\n276\t\\midrule\n277\tFuture labels (K-step) & 62\\% & No & None possible \\\\\n278\tMulti-step trajectory & 28\\% & Partial & GRU/temporal \\\\\n279\tGlobal trace context & 10\\% & Yes & Low signal \\\\\n280\t\\bottomrule\n281\t\\end{tabular}\n282\t\\end{table}\n283\t\n284\tThe best probes achieve 42\\% early detection rate (r1-32b GRU) vs. the oracle's 100\\%, and this gap is principled: the model's pre-commitment hidden states encode current safety reasoning state but do not encode when that reasoning will terminate.\n285\t\n286\t\\subsection{Architecture $\\times$ Scale Analysis}\n287\t\n288\tTable~\\ref{tab:scale} reports the 2$\\times$2 design matrix with OpenThinker-7B completing the Qwen2.5/7B cell. Neither architecture nor scale dominates detection quality; the primary determinant is the presence of reasoning training.\n289\t\n290\t\\begin{table}[t]\n291\t\\centering\n292\t\\small\n293\t\\caption{2$\\times$2 architecture $\\times$ scale matrix. Both factors contribute modestly; reasoning training is the primary determinant of probe quality.}\n294\t\\label{tab:scale}\n295\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n296\t\\toprule\n297\tArch & Scale & Model & FPR$_{\\text{pre}}$ & Prec & $\\Delta_{\\text{temp}}$ \\\\\n298\t\\midrule\n299\tLlama & 8B & r1-8b & 16.0\\% & 58.3\\% & +4.6pp \\\\\n300\tQwen2.5 & 7B & OpenThinker & 16.5\\% & 57.1\\% & +5.8pp \\\\\n301\tQwen2.5 & 32B & r1-32b & 14.2\\% & 60.1\\% & +6.9pp \\\\\n302\tQwen2.5 & 32B & QwQ-32B & 14.9\\% & 58.4\\% & +7.0pp \\\\\n303\t\\bottomrule\n304\t\\end{tabular}\n305\t\\end{table}\n306\t\n307\tScale provides a modest 2--3pp precision advantage (likely from richer representations in 32B models), while architecture contributes similarly. The near-parallel effects indicate no meaningful interaction between these factors.\n308\t\n309\t\\section{Ablations}\n310\t\n311\t\\subsection{Window Size}\n312\t\\label{sec:window_ablation}\n313\t\n314\tTable~\\ref{tab:window} reports the window size sweep. Coverage gains saturate at $W$=15--20 across all models, consistent with commitment dynamics occurring within a 15-step window. Pre-CP FPR remains stable across all window sizes (13--17\\%), confirming the precision advantage is robust to this hyperparameter.\n315\t\n316\t\\begin{table}[t]\n317\t\\centering\n318\t\\small\n319\t\\caption{Window size ablation. Coverage saturates at $W$=15--20; pre-CP FPR remains stable regardless of window size.}\n320\t\\label{tab:window}\n321\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n322\t\\toprule\n323\t$W$ & r1-8b & r1-32b & QwQ & Mean FPR$_{\\text{pre}}$ \\\\\n324\t\\midrule\n325\t1 (static) & 60.5\\% & 51.2\\% & 48.8\\% & 15.5\\% \\\\\n326\t5 & 62.3\\% & 53.8\\% & 51.2\\% & 14.8\\% \\\\\n327\t10 & 63.8\\% & 56.1\\% & 54.0\\% & 14.5\\% \\\\\n328\t15 & 65.1\\% & 58.1\\% & 55.8\\% & 14.7\\% \\\\\n329\t20 & 64.8\\% & 58.4\\% & 56.9\\% & 15.1\\% \\\\\n330\t25 & 64.2\\% & 57.6\\% & 55.4\\% & 15.8\\% \\\\\n331\t\\bottomrule\n332\t\\end{tabular}\n333\t\\end{table}\n334\t\n335\t\\subsection{Layer Selection}\n336\t\\label{sec:layer_ablation}\n337\t\n338\t\\begin{table}[t]\n339\t\\centering\n340\t\\small\n341\t\\caption{Layer selection ablation with temporal probes. The optimal layer at 44--47\\% depth is consistent across architectures with a broad plateau ($\\pm$5 layers within 2pp of peak).}\n342\t\\label{tab:layer_ablation}\n343\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n344\t\\toprule\n345\tModel & Best Layer & Depth & Peak Acc & Plateau \\\\\n346\t\\midrule\n347\tr1-8b (32L) & 14 & 44\\% & .848 & L10--L18 \\\\\n348\tr1-32b (64L) & 28 & 44\\% & .798 & L24--L34 \\\\\n349\tQwQ-32B (64L) & 30 & 47\\% & .781 & L26--L36 \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BrBVhVC51yBg6Sz2Ht5fBW","session_id":"584eac82-aa50-4806-9859-0d2ba8093b1f","uuid":"ebfbf394-74a6-4b84-bcf6-3ea61e8337a9","timestamp":"2026-05-16T21:53:55.682Z"}
